[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 1: The Move 5 Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/036e96df-2ab4-4f70-98f8-51a3655cc0c4/lines"]

1.e4 {I want to give you a second weapon for the Advance Caro–Kann: 4...e6. This move leads to a more solid type of gameplay than 4...Nc6. The choice is yours, depending on your style. In this section of bonus lines, you’ll find plenty of viable secondary weapons you can use against White, depending on your needs. Time for 1.e4, to which we respond with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. We immediately challenge it with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {The Advance Variation appears after 3.e5. We counterattack against d4 with 3...c5, striking back in the center.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White grabs the critical pawn on c5. In the 4...Nc6 line, we sometimes don’t get the pawn back for a while, which isn’t to everyone’s taste. After 4...e6, we’ll recover it soon enough.} 4...e6 5.f4 {White is defending the wrong pawn! At this point, 5.f4 is rare, it appears only about 3% of games. We respond by taking on c5.} 5...Bxc5 6.Nf3 {White develops logically. We do the same, also bringing our knight out.} 6...Nc6 7.Bd3 {White’s position isn’t bad, but it’s hard to castle kingside because of the premature f4. We make a flexible developing move, 7...Nh6, to keep our king safe after castling, and we’ll soon start breaking up White’s center with ...f6. Remember that ...Nh6 is a great move in most f4 setups.} 7...Nh6 8.Qe2 {White may try to trade bishops by protecting the e3 square with the queen, since our bishop on c5 is very strong. We simply castle.} 8...O-O 9.Be3 {White manages to place their bishop on e3, aiming to trade bishops. We accept the trade.} 9...Bxe3 10.Qxe3 {White recaptures with the queen.} 10...f6 {We'll go for our plan of undermining the center with ...f6.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 1: The Move 5 Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/036e96df-2ab4-4f70-98f8-51a3655cc0c4/lines"]

1.e4 {I want to give you a second weapon for the Advance Caro–Kann: 4...e6. This move leads to a more solid type of gameplay than 4...Nc6. The choice is yours, depending on your style. In this section of bonus lines, you’ll find plenty of viable secondary weapons you can use against White, depending on your needs. Time for 1.e4, to which we respond with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. We immediately challenge it with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {The Advance Variation appears after 3.e5. We counterattack against d4 with 3...c5, striking back in the center.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White grabs the critical pawn on c5. In the 4...Nc6 line, we sometimes don’t get the pawn back for a while, which isn’t to everyone’s taste. After 4...e6, we’ll recover it soon enough.} 4...e6 5.c4 {White attacks our center with 5.c4 but we can simply ignore this thrust and continue our development. It’s played in only about 4% of games. We immediately capture the c5 pawn with the bishop.} 5...Bxc5 6.cxd5 {Capturing on d5 seems to be White’s main idea. We recapture with our e-pawn.} 6...exd5 7.Nf3 {White keeps developing naturally. We do the same, with 7...Ne7. Our pieces come out easily.} 7...Ne7 8.Nc3 {Play continues with normal developing moves on both sides. We continue by developing our b-knight to c6.} 8...Nbc6 9.Be2 {White develops their light-squared bishop to e2, getting ready to castle.} 9...O-O {And we castle ourselves. We may even have ideas of ...d4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 1: The Move 5 Sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/036e96df-2ab4-4f70-98f8-51a3655cc0c4/lines"]

1.e4 {I want to give you a second weapon for the Advance Caro–Kann: 4...e6. This move leads to a more solid type of gameplay than 4...Nc6. The choice is yours, depending on your style. In this section of bonus lines, you’ll find plenty of viable secondary weapons you can use against White, depending on your needs. Time for 1.e4, to which we respond with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. We immediately challenge it with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {The Advance Variation appears after 3.e5. We counterattack against d4 with 3...c5, striking back in the center.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White grabs the critical pawn on c5. In the 4...Nc6 line, we sometimes don’t get the pawn back for a while, which isn’t to everyone’s taste. After 4...e6, we’ll recover it soon enough.} 4...e6 5.Bd3 {5.Bd3 is a natural developing move, but one we can exploit precisely. It’s quite rare, appearing in only about 1% of games. We’re in no rush to recapture on c5 just yet, and we prepare it with 5...Nd7.} 5...Nd7 6.Nf3 {White reinforces the e5 pawn with the knight, but this gives us a clear target - the bishop on d3. We challenge it immediately after capturing on c5 with the knight.} 6...Nxc5 7.O-O {White castles their king into safety.} 7...Ne7 {Maneuvering the other knight to c6 via e7 is a nice way to develop it around the annoying e5 pawn. We will take on d3, play ...Nc6 and ...Be7/Bd7 with a very solid position. Castling short and then playing on the queenside (...Rc8, ...Qb6) are plausible ideas.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 1: The Move 5 Sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/036e96df-2ab4-4f70-98f8-51a3655cc0c4/lines"]

1.e4 {I want to give you a second weapon for the Advance Caro–Kann: 4...e6. This move leads to a more solid type of gameplay than 4...Nc6. The choice is yours, depending on your style. In this section of bonus lines, you’ll find plenty of viable secondary weapons you can use against White, depending on your needs. Time for 1.e4, to which we respond with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. We immediately challenge it with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {The Advance Variation appears after 3.e5. We counterattack against d4 with 3...c5, striking back in the center.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White grabs the critical pawn on c5. In the 4...Nc6 line, we sometimes don’t get the pawn back for a while, which isn’t to everyone’s taste. After 4...e6, we’ll recover it soon enough.} 4...e6 5.Qg4 {White tries to stop us from recapturing on c5 with 5.Qg4, but we’ll use that exposed queen as a target. It’s a rare try, only about 1% of games, yet tricky enough to appear in practice. We respond with 5...h5, trying to chase the queen from attacking g7.} 5...h5 6.Qg3 {White insists on attacking our g7-pawn. So we keep chasing the queen, 6...h4.} 6...h4 7.Qg4 {White continues to insist, keeping pressure on g7. We respond by attacking the queen again, developing with tempo through 7...Nh6.} 7...Nh6 8.Bxh6 {If White insists on staying along the g-file then taking our knight is the only way. We recapture with the rook.} 8...Rxh6 9.Nf3 {White develops a piece, 9.Nf3, also pressuring our h4 pawn. But we gain another tempo by attacking the queen again. 9...f5 is our next move.} 9...f5 10.exf6 {White captures our f5 pawn en passant, removing the threat to the queen. We recapture on f6 with the queen, which now occupies an excellent square since the annoying e5 pawn is gone.} 10...Qxf6 11.Nc3 {White develops their knight, blocking the attack on b2.} 11...Bd7 {We calmly finish development with 11...Bd7, reaching a very promising position. We’ll capture on c5 with our bishop, play ...Nc6, and castle long, with the added possibility of ...e5, creating a discovered attack on the queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 1: The Move 5 Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/036e96df-2ab4-4f70-98f8-51a3655cc0c4/lines"]

1.e4 {I want to give you a second weapon for the Advance Caro–Kann: 4...e6. This move leads to a more solid type of gameplay than 4...Nc6. The choice is yours, depending on your style. In this section of bonus lines, you’ll find plenty of viable secondary weapons you can use against White, depending on your needs. Time for 1.e4, to which we respond with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. We immediately challenge it with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {The Advance Variation appears after 3.e5. We counterattack against d4 with 3...c5, striking back in the center.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White grabs the critical pawn on c5. In the 4...Nc6 line, we sometimes don’t get the pawn back for a while, which isn’t to everyone’s taste. After 4...e6, we’ll recover it soon enough.} 4...e6 5.b4 {Trying to hold onto the pawn with 5.b4 is ill-advised because White’s pawn chain is too vulnerable. Still, this mistake is common in online play - expect to see it around 17% of the time. We immediately begin softening up the pawn chain with 5...a5.} 5...a5 6.c3 {White tries to keep the chain together, but it collapses quickly. We capture on b4 without hesitation.} 6...axb4 7.cxb4 {The recapture on b4 is forced. Our key idea now makes White’s entire plan fall apart, 7...b6 is next.} 7...b6 8.Be3 {White keeps trying to defend, now with a bishop move, but it’s futile. We capture on c5, dismantling the pawn chain completely.} 8...bxc5 9.bxc5 {White captures back with the c-pawn. Instead, if 9. Bxc5 we recapture 9...Bxc5 10. bxc5.} 9...Nd7 {After 9...Nd7 the c5-pawn will fall shortly and once we complete our development in full we will be in great shape.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 2: The Modern 5. a3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/76b31fba-d14f-4e55-bbfb-5ca162c3270a/lines"]

1.e4 {No matter which line you choose against the 4.dxc5 Caro–Kann, this chapter is an important one. White’s setups with a3 have become topical and challenging over the past decade. The game starts with 1.e4, that means Caro time!} 1...c6 2.d4 {The game begins as usual. We build our typical light-square pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White plays the Advance Variation with 3.e5. We counter immediately against d4 in true Gotham style with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White takes on c5, the critical approach. As seen in the previous study, our idea is 4...e6, we'll get the pawn back soon enough.} 4...e6 5.a3 {A very nice idea from White, 5.a3, to prepare for our ...Bxc5 capture. White's goal is to gain a tempo with b4 and then play Bb2/Bd3 with good control over the center/kingside. This move is the fourth most popular, and expect to see it 14% of the time. It's the most popular among master games, though! Our solution is to develop our b-knight, attacking e5 and giving White a chance to go wrong.} 5...Nc6 6.Nf3 {The best way for White to protect e5 is 6.Nf3, played about 38% of the time. We immediately regain our pawn: 6...Bxc5 is next.} 6...Bxc5 7.b4 {White’s plan after 5.a3 was to push b4 once we recaptured on c5, and now it’s on the board with 7.b4. We calmly retreat our bishop to b6, escaping danger.} 7...Bb6 8.Bb2 {Moves like Bd3 and Bb2 are interchangeable, but they often appear in some order. We continue our development, 8...Nge7 is next.} 8...Nge7 9.Bd3 {Now we see White’s strategy unfold after 9.Bd3. We must block the dangerous bishop to prevent a potential Greek Gift sacrifice on h7, so we place our e7 knight on g6.} 9...Ng6 10.O-O {White castles their king. We also castle, bringing our king to safety.} 10...O-O 11.Re1 {White reinforces the powerful e5 pawn with the rook. We strike back with the ...f6 pawn break, aiming to free our pieces and challenge the center.} 11...f6 12.exf6 {White has to concede the space and captures on f6. We recapture with the g-pawn and gain a powerful central formation that forces White to react quickly.} 12...gxf6 13.c4 {13.c4 is definitely White’s best try to challenge our center. I like this straightforward approach, taking on c4, though the alternative 13...Nf4 14.Bf1 e5 is also interesting.} 13...dxc4 14.Bxc4 {White takes on c4 with the bishop. Our king is slightly exposed, so we trade queens. 14...Qxd1 is our move.} 14...Qxd1 15.Rxd1 {White recaptures with the rook. Our next idea is to get the king out of the pin with 15...Kg7, freeing ourselves to push the e-pawn next.} 15...Kg7 16.Nc3 {White continues with logical development, 16.Nc3.} 16...e5 {As mentioned, it's time for us to push our e-pawn, getting a strong central pawn formation and preparing to go for ...Bg4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 2: The Modern 5. a3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/76b31fba-d14f-4e55-bbfb-5ca162c3270a/lines"]

1.e4 {No matter which line you choose against the 4.dxc5 Caro–Kann, this chapter is an important one. White’s setups with a3 have become topical and challenging over the past decade. The game starts with 1.e4, that means Caro time!} 1...c6 2.d4 {The game begins as usual. We build our typical light-square pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White plays the Advance Variation with 3.e5. We counter immediately against d4 in true Gotham style with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White takes on c5, the critical approach. As seen in the previous study, our idea is 4...e6, we'll get the pawn back soon enough.} 4...e6 5.Nf3 {This is also a sound developing move. White can delay a3 for a moment. It’s the second most popular line, played in 19% of games. We simply regain our pawn with 5...Bxc5.} 5...Bxc5 6.Bd3 {White continues developing, still delaying a3 for now. You're going to see this move 16% of the time. We do the same, and our response is 6...Nc6.} 6...Nc6 7.O-O {Castling is a logical move. White can play a3 at any point which will take us to the main line. We also continue developing our pieces, 7...Nge7, getting ready to castle.} 7...Nge7 8.Bf4 {White goes for 8.Bf4, this being the most popular a3-less line. Our key move is 8...Ng6, which not only attacks f4 and e5 but also prevents any Greek Gift ideas (Bxh7+) after we castle.} 8...Ng6 9.Bg3 {White escapes the threat by retreating the bishop to g3. We castle, bringing our king to safety.} 9...O-O 10.Nbd2 {With 10.Nbd2, we reach a typical structure for this line. We have an interesting idea for counterplay here, 10...f5, gaining space and threatening to trap the bishop on g3.} 10...f5 11.exf6 {White takes en passant, stopping the advance of the f-pawn.} 11...gxf6 {Recapturing with the g-pawn is an interesting recapture. We weaken our king a bit but in turn get a powerful mobile pawn formation.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 2: The Modern 5. a3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/76b31fba-d14f-4e55-bbfb-5ca162c3270a/lines"]

1.e4 {No matter which line you choose against the 4.dxc5 Caro–Kann, this chapter is an important one. White’s setups with a3 have become topical and challenging over the past decade. The game starts with 1.e4, that means Caro time!} 1...c6 2.d4 {The game begins as usual. We build our typical light-square pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White plays the Advance Variation with 3.e5. We counter immediately against d4 in true Gotham style with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White takes on c5, the critical approach. As seen in the previous study, our idea is 4...e6, we'll get the pawn back soon enough.} 4...e6 5.a3 {A very nice idea from White, 5.a3, to prepare for our ...Bxc5 capture. White's goal is to gain a tempo with b4 and then play Bb2/Bd3 with good control over the center/kingside. This move is the fourth most popular, and expect to see it 14% of the time. It's the most popular among master games, though! Our solution is to develop our b-knight, attacking e5 and giving White a chance to go wrong.} 5...Nc6 6.f4 {White plays 6.f4, protecting the important e5 pawn. This move isn’t very logical and appears in only about 4% of games. 6.f4 actually helps our bishop after we capture on c5, so we do just that with 6...Bxc5, placing our bishop on an open diagonal.} 6...Bxc5 7.b4 {White stays consistent with their plan and attacks our bishop with 7.b4. As usual, we simply retreat to b6, keeping the bishop safe and well placed.} 7...Bb6 8.Nf3 {White continues developing with 8.Nf3.} 8...Nh6 {Our last move of the variation is a nice developing one, 8...Nh6. White will not find it easy to castle and we can launch a quick strike with ...f6 in the near future.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 2: The Modern 5. a3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/76b31fba-d14f-4e55-bbfb-5ca162c3270a/lines"]

1.e4 {No matter which line you choose against the 4.dxc5 Caro–Kann, this chapter is an important one. White’s setups with a3 have become topical and challenging over the past decade. The game starts with 1.e4, that means Caro time!} 1...c6 2.d4 {The game begins as usual. We build our typical light-square pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White plays the Advance Variation with 3.e5. We counter immediately against d4 in true Gotham style with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White takes on c5, the critical approach. As seen in the previous study, our idea is 4...e6, we'll get the pawn back soon enough.} 4...e6 5.a3 {A very nice idea from White, 5.a3, to prepare for our ...Bxc5 capture. White's goal is to gain a tempo with b4 and then play Bb2/Bd3 with good control over the center/kingside. This move is the fourth most popular, and expect to see it 14% of the time. It's the most popular among master games, though! Our solution is to develop our b-knight, attacking e5 and giving White a chance to go wrong.} 5...Nc6 6.b4 {This move looks consistent with a3 but it's mistimed because we can hammer at White's pawn chain. However, it's the most popular, played 52% of the time here! After 6...a5 White is going to have a hard time keeping the formation together.} 6...a5 7.b5 {Pushing the b-pawn again, attacking our knight, might look tempting. But we just get our pawn back by taking on e5 with the knight.} 7...Nxe5 8.Bb2 {White continues developing and places the bishop on the long diagonal - consistent, but the queenside pawns are overextended. We retreat the knight to d7, targeting the c5 pawn.} 8...Nd7 9.c6 {White pushes their c-pawn, the only way to keep it for now. We respond by taking on c6.} 9...bxc6 10.bxc6 {White recaptures on c6, attacking our knight.} 10...Ndf6 {Our knight's journey looks a little peculiar but we are ready to proceed with moves such as ...Qb6/Bc5/Ne7 and ...Ba6 if need be. The c6-pawn is going to fall.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 2: The Modern 5. a3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/76b31fba-d14f-4e55-bbfb-5ca162c3270a/lines"]

1.e4 {No matter which line you choose against the 4.dxc5 Caro–Kann, this chapter is an important one. White’s setups with a3 have become topical and challenging over the past decade. The game starts with 1.e4, that means Caro time!} 1...c6 2.d4 {The game begins as usual. We build our typical light-square pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White plays the Advance Variation with 3.e5. We counter immediately against d4 in true Gotham style with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White takes on c5, the critical approach. As seen in the previous study, our idea is 4...e6, we'll get the pawn back soon enough.} 4...e6 5.Nf3 {This is also a sound developing move. White can delay a3 for a moment. It’s the second most popular line, played in 19% of games. We simply regain our pawn with 5...Bxc5.} 5...Bxc5 6.Bb5+ {This dubious check has been surprisingly popular in online games, appearing in about 19% of them. We simply block while offering a trade of our least active piece.} 6...Bd7 7.Bxd7+ {White exchanges, trading a more active bishop for our less useful one. We recapture with development, 7...Nxd7.} 7...Nxd7 8.O-O {White castles their king.} 8...Ne7 {We continue developing with 8...Ne7, reaching a very harmonious position. We’ll castle short, improve our pieces with ...Ng6 to pressure e5, and place our rook on the semi-open c-file for active play.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 2: The Modern 5. a3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/76b31fba-d14f-4e55-bbfb-5ca162c3270a/lines"]

1.e4 {No matter which line you choose against the 4.dxc5 Caro–Kann, this chapter is an important one. White’s setups with a3 have become topical and challenging over the past decade. The game starts with 1.e4, that means Caro time!} 1...c6 2.d4 {The game begins as usual. We build our typical light-square pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White plays the Advance Variation with 3.e5. We counter immediately against d4 in true Gotham style with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White takes on c5, the critical approach. As seen in the previous study, our idea is 4...e6, we'll get the pawn back soon enough.} 4...e6 5.Nf3 {This is also a sound developing move. White can delay a3 for a moment. It’s the second most popular line, played in 19% of games. We simply regain our pawn with 5...Bxc5.} 5...Bxc5 6.a3 {This move just transposes directly into the main line.} 6...Nc6 {See the main line.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 3: The Main 5. Be3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/8603ac96-e54c-4616-a4c7-04a8e1932690/lines"]

1.e4 {Our final study of the chapter deals with the historically most common (and greediest!) idea: 5.Be3, where White tries to hold onto the extra pawn. White starts the game with 1.e4, best by test. As always our response is… the Caro.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. With 2...d5 we build our typical light-square pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White goes for the Advance Caro with 3.e5. Our solution doesn’t change - we counter the center with our Gotham-style antidote, 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White takes on c5, entering the greedy main line. As we’ve seen throughout this bonus-lines Advance Caro chapter, our remedy is 4...e6, opening up the dark-squared bishop to target c5.} 4...e6 5.Be3 {White develops the dark-squared bishop to e3, trying to keep the pawn. This move appears 28% of the time and is the most popular. We respond with 5...Nd7, insisting on recovering the pawn quickly.} 5...Nd7 6.Bb5 {The battle continues as White refuses to give up the pawn, pinning our knight to the king with 6.Bb5. This is by far the most common reply, played in half of all games. We develop the other knight to e7, preparing our key idea of ...Nf5 to destabilize the bishop on e3.} 6...Ne7 7.c3 {With 7.c3, White hopes to either push b4 or place the bishop on d4, creating a kind of mega pawn–bishop chain. You’ll see this move in about 19% of games. We continue with 7...a6, asking White to resolve the pin.} 7...a6 8.Bxd7+ {White captures on d7, trading the bishop for the knight. If White had played 8.Ba4 instead, then after ...Nc6 we’d be able to capture on e5 or c5. We recapture with our light-squared bishop.} 8...Bxd7 9.Nf3 {White develops naturally with 9.Nf3, getting just one move away from castling. Our whole plan comes to life with 9...Nf5, targeting the dark-squared bishop and opening up our own bishop’s scope.} 9...Nf5 10.Bd4 {White plants the bishop on d4, sticking to the setup - and it looks like we’ll remain a pawn down. But we activate with ...Rc8, still aiming to attack the c5 pawn.} 10...Rc8 11.b4 {White’s only way to defend c5 is 11.b4. We now unleash a very nice pawn sacrifice with 11...b6. We’ll be down a pawn, but our active pieces and White’s weakened queenside give us ample compensation.} 11...b6 12.cxb6 {White captures on b6 - otherwise we win c5 back easily. First we eliminate the key defender with 12...Nxd4, clearing the way to recapture on b6.} 12...Nxd4 13.Qxd4 {White recaptures with the queen, still trying to hold everything together.} 13...Rc4 {We finish with 13...Rc4, a precise move kicking away the queen. Our active pieces, queenside targets, and bishop pair give us full compensation once we take on b6. We can even toss in ...Re4+ to make White lose castling rights.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 3: The Main 5. Be3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/8603ac96-e54c-4616-a4c7-04a8e1932690/lines"]

1.e4 {Our final study of the chapter deals with the historically most common (and greediest!) idea: 5.Be3, where White tries to hold onto the extra pawn. White starts the game with 1.e4, best by test. As always our response is… the Caro.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. With 2...d5 we build our typical light-square pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White goes for the Advance Caro with 3.e5. Our solution doesn’t change - we counter the center with our Gotham-style antidote, 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White takes on c5, entering the greedy main line. As we’ve seen throughout this bonus-lines Advance Caro chapter, our remedy is 4...e6, opening up the dark-squared bishop to target c5.} 4...e6 5.Be3 {White develops the dark-squared bishop to e3, trying to keep the pawn. This move appears 28% of the time and is the most popular. We respond with 5...Nd7, insisting on recovering the pawn quickly.} 5...Nd7 6.Nf3 {White gives up on the dream of keeping the extra pawn and simply develops with 6.Nf3. This isn’t a bad move, but it’s nothing to be afraid of - you’ll see it about 18% of the time. We immediately capture on c5 with the bishop, restoring material equality.} 6...Bxc5 7.Bxc5 {White trades bishops by taking on c5. We recapture with the knight.} 7...Nxc5 8.Qd4 {8.Qd4 is the most played move among experienced players. Most other moves can be met with the simple ...Ne7 and ...0-0, along with ...Bd7/Rc8. Here we defend our knight actively with the queen. 8...Qb6 is our move.} 8...Qb6 9.Nbd2 {White develops with 9.Nbd2, keeping the option of castling long. We also develop with 9...Ne7, not only preparing to castle but also getting ready to attack the queen.} 9...Ne7 10.Nb3 {White plays 10.Nb3, pressuring our knight on c5 and aiming to open the a-file for the rook. We’re forced to take on b3.} 10...Nxb3 11.axb3 {White recaptures with the a-pawn. We trade queens with 11...Qxd4, an equal and natural exchange.} 11...Qxd4 12.Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight. We play 12...Ng6, putting pressure on e5 and forcing White onto the defensive.} 12...Ng6 13.Nf3 {White retreats the knight to f3, a necessary move to support the e5 pawn.} 13...f6 {Now comes 13...f6! White is forced to trade, and after the exchanges we’ll achieve an excellent position with a strong, healthy center.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 3: The Main 5. Be3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/8603ac96-e54c-4616-a4c7-04a8e1932690/lines"]

1.e4 {Our final study of the chapter deals with the historically most common (and greediest!) idea: 5.Be3, where White tries to hold onto the extra pawn. White starts the game with 1.e4, best by test. As always our response is… the Caro.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. With 2...d5 we build our typical light-square pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White goes for the Advance Caro with 3.e5. Our solution doesn’t change - we counter the center with our Gotham-style antidote, 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White takes on c5, entering the greedy main line. As we’ve seen throughout this bonus-lines Advance Caro chapter, our remedy is 4...e6, opening up the dark-squared bishop to target c5.} 4...e6 5.Be3 {White develops the dark-squared bishop to e3, trying to keep the pawn. This move appears 28% of the time and is the most popular. We respond with 5...Nd7, insisting on recovering the pawn quickly.} 5...Nd7 6.Bb5 {The battle continues as White refuses to give up the pawn, pinning our knight to the king with 6.Bb5. This is by far the most common reply, played in half of all games. We develop the other knight to e7, preparing our key idea of ...Nf5 to destabilize the bishop on e3.} 6...Ne7 7.Nf3 {White develops naturally, 7.Nf3, but this doesn’t stop our ...Nf5 idea - it’s played 38% of the time. So we execute our plan with 7...Nf5, targeting the bishop on e3.} 7...Nf5 8.Bd4 {Planting the bishop on d4 to keep everything protected is not the best, but it is the most common response. It’s time for us to give a check with the queen, 8...Qa5+, preventing any b4 ideas and adding more pressure to c5.} 8...Qa5+ 9.Nc3 {White is forced to block with the knight to avoid losing the b5-bishop. We now get rid of the pin. 9...a6 comes next.} 9...a6 10.Bxd7+ {White captures on d7, giving up the bishop for the knight. If White retreats instead, we simply take on c5. We recapture and develop at the same time, 10...Bxd7.} 10...Bxd7 11.O-O {White castles short - there’s nothing better. Before taking on c5, we grab the bishop pair, so we take on d4.} 11...Nxd4 12.Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight, the logical move.} 12...Qxc5 {After 12...Qxc5 we’ve recovered the pawn and kept the bishop pair. We’ll castle next and begin queenside play, and a timely ...f6 can be strong to open diagonals for our bishops.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 3: The Main 5. Be3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/8603ac96-e54c-4616-a4c7-04a8e1932690/lines"]

1.e4 {Our final study of the chapter deals with the historically most common (and greediest!) idea: 5.Be3, where White tries to hold onto the extra pawn. White starts the game with 1.e4, best by test. As always our response is… the Caro.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. With 2...d5 we build our typical light-square pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White goes for the Advance Caro with 3.e5. Our solution doesn’t change - we counter the center with our Gotham-style antidote, 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White takes on c5, entering the greedy main line. As we’ve seen throughout this bonus-lines Advance Caro chapter, our remedy is 4...e6, opening up the dark-squared bishop to target c5.} 4...e6 5.Be3 {White develops the dark-squared bishop to e3, trying to keep the pawn. This move appears 28% of the time and is the most popular. We respond with 5...Nd7, insisting on recovering the pawn quickly.} 5...Nd7 6.Bb5 {The battle continues as White refuses to give up the pawn, pinning our knight to the king with 6.Bb5. This is by far the most common reply, played in half of all games. We develop the other knight to e7, preparing our key idea of ...Nf5 to destabilize the bishop on e3.} 6...Ne7 7.Nf3 {White develops naturally, 7.Nf3, but this doesn’t stop our ...Nf5 idea - it’s played 38% of the time. So we execute our plan with 7...Nf5, targeting the bishop on e3.} 7...Nf5 8.b4 {Playing 8.b4 looks ugly, but it’s the only way for White to hang on to the extra pawn at this point. We’re happy to grab the bishop and double White’s pawns, 8...Nxe3 is our next move.} 8...Nxe3 9.fxe3 {White recaptures, forced to accept the doubled pawns. Next we strike with 9...a5!, attacking the pawn chain immediately.} 9...a5 10.c3 {White reinforces with 10.c3, necessary to defend b4. We simply continue developing with 10...Be7.} 10...Be7 11.O-O {White castles short, bringing the king to safety.} 11...O-O {We do the same. Our kingside development is complete, and we’re ready to play ...Qc7 to pressure both e5 and c5. We can also prepare ...b6 later to open more files and diagonals for our pieces. White may be up a pawn, but with all their pawns weak, we have excellent compensation.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Variation (4...e6) / Study 3: The Main 5. Be3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/ab76dad8-a2ce-438d-8f53-efbc2a513fb7/studies/8603ac96-e54c-4616-a4c7-04a8e1932690/lines"]

1.e4 {Our final study of the chapter deals with the historically most common (and greediest!) idea: 5.Be3, where White tries to hold onto the extra pawn. White starts the game with 1.e4, best by test. As always our response is… the Caro.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. With 2...d5 we build our typical light-square pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White goes for the Advance Caro with 3.e5. Our solution doesn’t change - we counter the center with our Gotham-style antidote, 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White takes on c5, entering the greedy main line. As we’ve seen throughout this bonus-lines Advance Caro chapter, our remedy is 4...e6, opening up the dark-squared bishop to target c5.} 4...e6 5.Be3 {White develops the dark-squared bishop to e3, trying to keep the pawn. This move appears 28% of the time and is the most popular. We respond with 5...Nd7, insisting on recovering the pawn quickly.} 5...Nd7 6.b4 {6.b4 is a mistake, as White is giving up an important central pawn. And this mistake is played 17% of the time! We immediately regain the pawn with the knight, restoring material equality.} 6...Nxe5 7.Nf3 {White may offer a trade of knights. We accept it with 7...Nxf3+.} 7...Nxf3+ 8.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen. We follow up with 8...a5, a common and thematic undermining push.} 8...a5 9.c3 {White defends the pawn with 9.c3.} 9...Ne7 {We finish the variation with 9...Ne7. The knight will soon jump to an attractive square like c6 or f5 before we complete kingside development.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Exchange Variation (5...g6) / Study 1: G6 Setups With F6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/cef2c0aa-9b7a-4e2c-b2cb-81d039ff3920/studies/26d5f4f9-c844-4194-ad29-c938de7daffd/lines"]

1.e4 {This is a bonus study where I want to share an interesting and rarer setup against the Exchange Variation. If you’re looking for a more aggressive and surprising reaction, this is it! White starts the game with 1.e4 - so far so good. As always, we respond with the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center, 2.d4. We continue with 2...d5, exactly what the doctor ordered.} 2...d5 {Exactly what the doctor ordered.} 3.exd5 {White captures on d5, entering the Exchange Variation. We recapture with the c-pawn, keeping a pawn in the center and reaching the classic Carlsbad structure.} 3...cxd5 4.Bd3 {White goes for the main line. with 4.Bd3. Other deviations were dealt with in the main Caro-Kann Course. We immediately challenge the d4 pawn by playing 4...Nc6.} 4...Nc6 5.c3 {After 5.c3, we introduce our twist with an unusual move, 5...g6! The main idea is to prepare ...Bf5 while opening the g-file for potential attacking chances. Another key concept is to control the e5 square with ...Bg7 and ...f6.} 5...g6 6.Bf4 {If White starts with Bf4, that gives us extra motivation to push ...e5 with tempo. In the next study, we’ll look at 6.Nf3. For now, we continue with normal development, 6...Bg7 is next.} 6...Bg7 7.Nf3 {White follows suit with 7.Nf3 in about 48% of games. We respond with the unusual yet thematic 7...Nh6! This entire system is unconventional: this move supports the f5 square for ...Bf5 and prepares the surprising ...Nf7 retreat, supporting a later ...e5.} 7...Nh6 8.O-O {White reacts calmly, bringing their king to safety. We castle as well.} 8...O-O 9.Re1 {White plays 9.Re1, controlling the e5 square. As mentioned, the safest move is ...Bf5, since after 9...f6, White can reply with the strong 10.c4!. However, we have a creative idea to prepare ...f6. First, we safeguard the king by tucking it into the corner with 9...Kh8, and only then do we play ...f6 with the king better placed.} 9...Kh8 10.Nbd2 {White’s reply is logical, developing the b-knight to d2. We now execute our plan. The ...f6–Nf7 maneuver comes next.} 10...f6 11.h3 {White creates luft for the king with 11.h3. We continue with 11...Nf7, preparing to push e5.} 11...Nf7 12.Bh2 {White retreats the bishop to h2, trying to avoid losing a tempo after ...e5. But we play it anyway, seizing control of the center.} 12...e5 13.dxe5 {White takes on e5, since they need to avoid getting forked. We recapture with the f-pawn, maintaining a strong center.} 13...fxe5 {We recapture with the f-pawn, maintaining a strong center} 14.Bb5 {White escapes the fork threat with 14.Bb5 and tries to win the e5 pawn.} 14...e4 {But we strike back in the center with 14...e4. Our position becomes extremely powerful, with all our pieces ready for action.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Exchange Variation (5...g6) / Study 1: G6 Setups With F6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/cef2c0aa-9b7a-4e2c-b2cb-81d039ff3920/studies/26d5f4f9-c844-4194-ad29-c938de7daffd/lines"]

1.e4 {This is a bonus study where I want to share an interesting and rarer setup against the Exchange Variation. If you’re looking for a more aggressive and surprising reaction, this is it! White starts the game with 1.e4 - so far so good. As always, we respond with the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center, 2.d4. We continue with 2...d5, exactly what the doctor ordered.} 2...d5 {Exactly what the doctor ordered.} 3.exd5 {White captures on d5, entering the Exchange Variation. We recapture with the c-pawn, keeping a pawn in the center and reaching the classic Carlsbad structure.} 3...cxd5 4.Bd3 {White goes for the main line. with 4.Bd3. Other deviations were dealt with in the main Caro-Kann Course. We immediately challenge the d4 pawn by playing 4...Nc6.} 4...Nc6 5.c3 {After 5.c3, we introduce our twist with an unusual move, 5...g6! The main idea is to prepare ...Bf5 while opening the g-file for potential attacking chances. Another key concept is to control the e5 square with ...Bg7 and ...f6.} 5...g6 6.Bf4 {If White starts with Bf4, that gives us extra motivation to push ...e5 with tempo. In the next study, we’ll look at 6.Nf3. For now, we continue with normal development, 6...Bg7 is next.} 6...Bg7 7.Nf3 {White follows suit with 7.Nf3 in about 48% of games. We respond with the unusual yet thematic 7...Nh6! This entire system is unconventional: this move supports the f5 square for ...Bf5 and prepares the surprising ...Nf7 retreat, supporting a later ...e5.} 7...Nh6 8.O-O {White reacts calmly, bringing their king to safety. We castle as well.} 8...O-O 9.Nbd2 {Now comes an important moment. White plays 9.Nbd2, blocking the queen’s vision along the d-file. This allows us to strike with the powerful 9...f6! If White had played 9.Re1 instead, ...f6 would be riskier due to the strong 10.c4! followed by Nc3. In that case, 9...Bf5 would be safer, planning to recapture with the knight.} 9...f6 10.Re1 {White slides the rook to the semi-open e-file, trying to clamp down on e5, but it’s already too late.} 10...Nf7 {We end the variation by bringing our knight back to f7, reaching a great position - the ...e5 break is now completely unstoppable.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Exchange Variation (5...g6) / Study 1: G6 Setups With F6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/cef2c0aa-9b7a-4e2c-b2cb-81d039ff3920/studies/26d5f4f9-c844-4194-ad29-c938de7daffd/lines"]

1.e4 {This is a bonus study where I want to share an interesting and rarer setup against the Exchange Variation. If you’re looking for a more aggressive and surprising reaction, this is it! White starts the game with 1.e4 - so far so good. As always, we respond with the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center, 2.d4. We continue with 2...d5, exactly what the doctor ordered.} 2...d5 {Exactly what the doctor ordered.} 3.exd5 {White captures on d5, entering the Exchange Variation. We recapture with the c-pawn, keeping a pawn in the center and reaching the classic Carlsbad structure.} 3...cxd5 4.Bd3 {White goes for the main line. with 4.Bd3. Other deviations were dealt with in the main Caro-Kann Course. We immediately challenge the d4 pawn by playing 4...Nc6.} 4...Nc6 5.c3 {After 5.c3, we introduce our twist with an unusual move, 5...g6! The main idea is to prepare ...Bf5 while opening the g-file for potential attacking chances. Another key concept is to control the e5 square with ...Bg7 and ...f6.} 5...g6 6.Bf4 {If White starts with Bf4, that gives us extra motivation to push ...e5 with tempo. In the next study, we’ll look at 6.Nf3. For now, we continue with normal development, 6...Bg7 is next.} 6...Bg7 7.h3 {If White tries to stop ...Bg4 ideas with 7.h3 before playing Nf3, we have a rude surprise. This move appears in about 8% of games. After 7...f6, there’s suddenly no way to stop ...e5, and we’ll completely dominate the center.} 7...f6 8.Ne2 {Suppose White develops with 8.Ne2 - 8.Nf3 looks better, but then ...e5! followed by ...e4! creates a powerful fork. With 8...e5, we establish a dominating center.} 8...e5 9.Bh2 {White goes back to h2 with their dark-squared bishop, a sad retreat.} 9...Nge7 {We have a much better position because our pawns are dominating the board. Our last move is developing our g-knight to e7, getting ready to castle. The light-squared bishop can develop to e6, and our center stands strong.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Exchange Variation (5...g6) / Study 2: G6 Setups with Bf5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/cef2c0aa-9b7a-4e2c-b2cb-81d039ff3920/studies/01c8f7fe-7469-4a34-996a-972c43134c1e/lines"]

1.e4 {In the previous study, we looked at the aggressive ...f6 and ...e5 ideas, but as I mentioned, the move ...g6 also serves to facilitate ...Bf5. Let’s see how that looks in practice. White starts with the familiar 1.e4 formula. We reply with the Gotham Caro, 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center. We love the center just as much, so we counter with 2...d5, building our typical light-squared pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {The Exchange Variation appears after 3.exd5. We recapture in our standard way, 3...cxd5, keeping a solid structure.} 3...cxd5 4.Bd3 {The main line continues. We develop our knight to c6, immediately attacking the pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.c3 {White defends the pawn by playing 5.c3. Now it’s time to explore our ...Bf5 ideas. 5...g6 comes next.} 5...g6 6.Nf3 {White develops the knight and prepares to castle - a good plan. Without hesitation, we offer the trade, 6...Bf5 is our next move.} 6...Bf5 7.Bxf5 {White may be tempted to double our pawns by trading on f5 - and indeed does so in more than 51% of games. We’re forced to recapture with the g-pawn, but in return we gain strong control over the e4 square and an open g-file for future pressure.} 7...gxf5 8.Bf4 {White continues developing normally with 8.Bf4. We answer with 8...e6, building a “tree” pawn setup on the light squares and preparing to develop our bishop to d6.} 8...e6 9.Nbd2 {White reasonably continues developing with 9.Nbd2. We also develop with 9...Bd6, gaining a tempo by attacking the dark-squared bishop.} 9...Bd6 10.Bxd6 {White trades on d6. We’re forced to recapture with the queen.} 10...Qxd6 11.O-O {White castles short, a natural move, but it gives us a clear target, especially with the open g-file at our disposal.} 11...Nf6 {We finish the variation with 11...Nf6, and I really like our position here. We’ll follow up with ...Ne4 and then ...Rg8 becomes highly tempting. Our king can safely tuck away on the queenside, while White will face enormous pressure along the open g-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Exchange Variation (5...g6) / Study 2: G6 Setups with Bf5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/cef2c0aa-9b7a-4e2c-b2cb-81d039ff3920/studies/01c8f7fe-7469-4a34-996a-972c43134c1e/lines"]

1.e4 {In the previous study, we looked at the aggressive ...f6 and ...e5 ideas, but as I mentioned, the move ...g6 also serves to facilitate ...Bf5. Let’s see how that looks in practice. White starts with the familiar 1.e4 formula. We reply with the Gotham Caro, 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center. We love the center just as much, so we counter with 2...d5, building our typical light-squared pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {The Exchange Variation appears after 3.exd5. We recapture in our standard way, 3...cxd5, keeping a solid structure.} 3...cxd5 4.Bd3 {The main line continues. We develop our knight to c6, immediately attacking the pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.c3 {White defends the pawn by playing 5.c3. Now it’s time to explore our ...Bf5 ideas. 5...g6 comes next.} 5...g6 6.Nf3 {White develops the knight and prepares to castle - a good plan. Without hesitation, we offer the trade, 6...Bf5 is our next move.} 6...Bf5 7.O-O {Chess isn’t checkers, so White isn’t obligated to capture on f5 and instead castles. This happens about 33% of the time. We make the trade ourselves with 7...Bxd3.} 7...Bxd3 8.Qxd3 {White is forced to recapture with the queen. We simply continue developing with 8...Bg7.} 8...Bg7 9.Bf4 {White develops as well, bringing the dark-squared bishop out to f4. We continue with kingside development, bringing the undeveloped knight to c6 - now only one move away from castling.} 9...Nf6 10.Nbd2 {White plays another standard developing move, 10.Nbd2. As mentioned, our next move is to castle - safe and sound.} 10...O-O 11.h3 {White chooses 11.h3, creating an escape square for the king and bishop. It’s not a forced move, but the game would continue similarly either way.} 11...e6 {We end the variation with 11...e6, building our pawns on strong light squares. Not only is this a typical Caro–Kann idea, but it’s also logical since we’ve traded our light-squared bishop. From here, we should aim to play on the queenside with ideas like ...Rc8 and ...Na5–Nc4. A timely ...Qb6 can also be annoying for White, and we can reroute the knight from f6 to e8–d6 or back to d7 to control key squares such as e5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Classical Variation (4...Bf5) / Study 1: Deviations to the Main Line Part 1: Early Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/b1f9a133-3c74-4b87-b12e-7c906e233da6/studies/60e54525-56c7-4126-8191-16550f8c28aa/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of our second weapon against the Classical Caro. Here we will analyze the historic Capablanca Variation (4...Bf5), which long served as the main line before the discoveries hidden in the Tartakower (4...Nf6). I’ve used this variation extensively over the years and am excited to share its ideas and nuances with you. The game begins with 1.e4. We reply with 1...c6, preparing the Caro pawn chain.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White classically controls the center with 2.d4. As always, we challenge it directly with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, entering the Classical Variation. Our solution is to capture on e4, ceding some central space in exchange for smooth development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. As mentioned in the introduction, instead of playing the Tartakower, we choose 4...Bf5, entering the Capablanca Variation. In this line, we aim to develop our pieces efficiently and without concessions.} 4...Bf5 5.Ng3 {Retreating the knight to g3 with tempo is White’s best and most popular option, played a massive 76% of the time. We simply retreat our bishop to g6, keeping it active on a strong diagonal.} 5...Bg6 6.Nf3 {One of White’s most common moves is the developing move 6.Nf3. The main line with 6.h4 will be addressed in Studies 3 and 4, while the knight-to-f4 plans (via Nh3 or Ne2) will be studied next. This move occurs in roughly half of all games in this position. We continue developing with 6...Nd7, controlling the e5 square and limiting White’s knight jumps. Although 7.h4 is the most common continuation, here we focus on White’s approaches without the h4 push.} 6...Nd7 7.Bc4 {White can also try to place their bishop more actively with 7.Bc4, allowing our Bg6 to stay on the board. We blunt the bishop and prepare development with 7...e6.} 7...e6 8.O-O {White castles, a natural move in this position. We continue developing strongly with 8...Bd6, placing our bishop on an active diagonal.} 8...Bd6 9.c3 {White plays 9.c3, reinforcing d4, one of several natural options.} 9...Ngf6 {We finish the line with 9...Ngf6, reaching our typical Classical Caro setup. We are ready to castle and eventually push our queenside pawns for counterplay, potentially after Qc7 and Rd8.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Classical Variation (4...Bf5) / Study 1: Deviations to the Main Line Part 1: Early Sidelines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/b1f9a133-3c74-4b87-b12e-7c906e233da6/studies/60e54525-56c7-4126-8191-16550f8c28aa/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of our second weapon against the Classical Caro. Here we will analyze the historic Capablanca Variation (4...Bf5), which long served as the main line before the discoveries hidden in the Tartakower (4...Nf6). I’ve used this variation extensively over the years and am excited to share its ideas and nuances with you. The game begins with 1.e4. We reply with 1...c6, preparing the Caro pawn chain.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White classically controls the center with 2.d4. As always, we challenge it directly with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, entering the Classical Variation. Our solution is to capture on e4, ceding some central space in exchange for smooth development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. As mentioned in the introduction, instead of playing the Tartakower, we choose 4...Bf5, entering the Capablanca Variation. In this line, we aim to develop our pieces efficiently and without concessions.} 4...Bf5 5.Qf3 {Among all the unusual options on move five, 5.Qf3 actually has some merit. White’s idea is simply to trade our Bf5 for a knight, even though the move is played only 1% of the time. We solve the problem with 5...e6, defending the bishop and preparing development.} 5...e6 6.Ne2 {White plays 6.Ne2, protecting d4 and aiming for N2g3. Other moves like c3 or Be3 can be met with the simple ...Nd7–Ngf6. We continue with 6...Nd7, calmly developing; 6...Be7 was also fine. Even if we eventually trade our active Bf5, our structure remains solid with easy development ahead.} 6...Nd7 7.N2g3 {White attacks our bishop with 7.N2g3. We can retreat to g6, although trading on e4 immediately is also acceptable. ...Bg6 is also an idea to provoke h4–h5.} 7...Bg6 8.h4 {White plays h4, trying to harass our bishop. We develop our kingside by bringing out our other knight to f6.} 8...Ngf6 9.h5 {White continues with 9.h5, attacking the bishop. Now that the h-pawn is committed, we take on e4, trading our bishop for the knight.} 9...Bxe4 10.Nxe4 {White recaptures with the knight. We accept the exchange and capture on e4 again.} 10...Nxe4 11.Qxe4 {The queen recaptures. We improve our remaining knight by placing it on f6, gaining a tempo on the queen.} 11...Nf6 12.Qe3 {The queen steps out of danger and moves to e3.} 12...h6 {We now play 12...h6, preventing White from playing that move themselves. We can continue with ...Qd5, ...Be7, and castling.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Classical Variation (4...Bf5) / Study 1: Deviations to the Main Line Part 1: Early Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/b1f9a133-3c74-4b87-b12e-7c906e233da6/studies/60e54525-56c7-4126-8191-16550f8c28aa/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of our second weapon against the Classical Caro. Here we will analyze the historic Capablanca Variation (4...Bf5), which long served as the main line before the discoveries hidden in the Tartakower (4...Nf6). I’ve used this variation extensively over the years and am excited to share its ideas and nuances with you. The game begins with 1.e4. We reply with 1...c6, preparing the Caro pawn chain.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White classically controls the center with 2.d4. As always, we challenge it directly with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, entering the Classical Variation. Our solution is to capture on e4, ceding some central space in exchange for smooth development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. As mentioned in the introduction, instead of playing the Tartakower, we choose 4...Bf5, entering the Capablanca Variation. In this line, we aim to develop our pieces efficiently and without concessions.} 4...Bf5 5.Ng3 {Retreating the knight to g3 with tempo is White’s best and most popular option, played a massive 76% of the time. We simply retreat our bishop to g6, keeping it active on a strong diagonal.} 5...Bg6 6.Nf3 {One of White’s most common moves is the developing move 6.Nf3. The main line with 6.h4 will be addressed in Studies 3 and 4, while the knight-to-f4 plans (via Nh3 or Ne2) will be studied next. This move occurs in roughly half of all games in this position. We continue developing with 6...Nd7, controlling the e5 square and limiting White’s knight jumps. Although 7.h4 is the most common continuation, here we focus on White’s approaches without the h4 push.} 6...Nd7 7.Bd3 {White’s most common idea in this setup is to offer the bishop trade with 7.Bd3. We don’t trade and instead maintain the tension by developing our g-knight to f6, keeping the option for White to open the h-file for our rook if they decide to capture.} 7...Ngf6 8.O-O {White castles short, a natural and safe developing move. We continue with the typical Caro idea of 8...e6, opening the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop.} 8...e6 9.Re1 {White activates the rook, placing it on the semi-open e-file and aligning it with our king.} 9...Be7 {Our final move is 9...Be7, calmly developing and preparing to castle. In the future, we should aim for queenside play via a ...c5 break, or even a space-gaining ...a5–a4 thrust (especially if White plays b3–Bb2). In general, both sides have a very solid and sound position, with White enjoying slightly more space - which is why ...c5 is a key idea to regain that space, often supported by ...Qc7 or ...Qb6 and ...Rd8 or ...Rc8.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Classical Variation (4...Bf5) / Study 1: Deviations to the Main Line Part 1: Early Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/b1f9a133-3c74-4b87-b12e-7c906e233da6/studies/60e54525-56c7-4126-8191-16550f8c28aa/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of our second weapon against the Classical Caro. Here we will analyze the historic Capablanca Variation (4...Bf5), which long served as the main line before the discoveries hidden in the Tartakower (4...Nf6). I’ve used this variation extensively over the years and am excited to share its ideas and nuances with you. The game begins with 1.e4. We reply with 1...c6, preparing the Caro pawn chain.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White classically controls the center with 2.d4. As always, we challenge it directly with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, entering the Classical Variation. Our solution is to capture on e4, ceding some central space in exchange for smooth development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. As mentioned in the introduction, instead of playing the Tartakower, we choose 4...Bf5, entering the Capablanca Variation. In this line, we aim to develop our pieces efficiently and without concessions.} 4...Bf5 5.Ng3 {Retreating the knight to g3 with tempo is White’s best and most popular option, played a massive 76% of the time. We simply retreat our bishop to g6, keeping it active on a strong diagonal.} 5...Bg6 6.Nf3 {One of White’s most common moves is the developing move 6.Nf3. The main line with 6.h4 will be addressed in Studies 3 and 4, while the knight-to-f4 plans (via Nh3 or Ne2) will be studied next. This move occurs in roughly half of all games in this position. We continue developing with 6...Nd7, controlling the e5 square and limiting White’s knight jumps. Although 7.h4 is the most common continuation, here we focus on White’s approaches without the h4 push.} 6...Nd7 7.Nh4 {White can also chase our bishop with 7.Nh4. We respond by opening the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop and queen, which now targets the knight.} 7...e6 8.Nxg6 {White trades on g6, winning the bishop pair. That was White’s idea all along.} 8...hxg6 {We recapture with our h-pawn. In exchange for our good bishop, we gain a powerful rook file, and our development will flow naturally with Ngf6, Bd6, Qc7, and potentially 0-0-0.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Classical Variation (4...Bf5) / Study 1: Deviations to the Main Line Part 1: Early Sidelines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/b1f9a133-3c74-4b87-b12e-7c906e233da6/studies/60e54525-56c7-4126-8191-16550f8c28aa/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of our second weapon against the Classical Caro. Here we will analyze the historic Capablanca Variation (4...Bf5), which long served as the main line before the discoveries hidden in the Tartakower (4...Nf6). I’ve used this variation extensively over the years and am excited to share its ideas and nuances with you. The game begins with 1.e4. We reply with 1...c6, preparing the Caro pawn chain.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White classically controls the center with 2.d4. As always, we challenge it directly with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, entering the Classical Variation. Our solution is to capture on e4, ceding some central space in exchange for smooth development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. As mentioned in the introduction, instead of playing the Tartakower, we choose 4...Bf5, entering the Capablanca Variation. In this line, we aim to develop our pieces efficiently and without concessions.} 4...Bf5 5.f3 {White can choose 5.f3, an anti-positional move that’s popular online but simply bad. White can’t develop Nf3, and the king on e1 becomes slightly loose. We continue with simple development, 5...e6, threatening to capture on e4 combined with …Qh4+; the plan with Nd7–Nf6 is also strong.} 5...e6 6.Bf4 {Strong players will cover the dark squares with 6.Bf4. Here we simply propose a knight trade with 6...Nf6.} 6...Nf6 7.Nxf6+ {White decides to trade on f6. We recapture with the queen; if 8.Be5, then 8...Qg6 followed by ...Nd7 is better for Black.} 7...Qxf6 8.Ne2 {The move f3 makes development awkward, forcing the knight to come out via e2, which blocks the light-squared bishop. We slide our queen to g6, targeting c2.} 8...Qg6 9.c3 {White defends the pawn by pushing it one square.} 9...Nd7 {We finish the line with 9...Nd7. As Black, we’ll have the option to castle queenside or kingside. Just remember: if h4, play …h5, and if Ng3, play …Bc2.} *

