1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of our second weapon against the Classical Caro. Here we will analyze the historic Capablanca Variation (4...Bf5), which long served as the main line before the discoveries hidden in the Tartakower (4...Nf6). I’ve used this variation extensively over the years and am excited to share its ideas and nuances with you. The game begins with 1.e4. We reply with 1...c6, preparing the Caro pawn chain.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White classically controls the center with 2.d4. As always, we challenge it directly with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, entering the Classical Variation. Our solution is to capture on e4, ceding some central space in exchange for smooth development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. As mentioned in the introduction, instead of playing the Tartakower, we choose 4...Bf5, entering the Capablanca Variation. In this line, we aim to develop our pieces efficiently and without concessions.} 4...Bf5 5.Nc5 {The first variation of this study appears with 5.Nc5, where White gains a tempo on the b7 pawn instead of retreating. This move is played only 2% of the time. Our solution is 5...e5, striking the center with an aggressive reaction; 5...b6 was also simple, followed by ...e6, ...Nd7, ...Bd6, and ...Ne7.} 5...e5 6.Nxb7 {White takes the pawn on b7, the point of Nc5. We now play 6...Qb6, attacking the knight.} 6...Qb6 7.Nc5 {White’s only retreat is back to c5. We capture on c5 with the bishop, looking to regain the pawn while developing.} 7...Bxc5 8.dxc5 {White recaptures the piece.} 8...Qxc5 {We recapture with the queen, restoring material balance. We are ahead in development and will continue with ...Nf6 and ...0-0. If White tries 9.Bd3, then e4! is strong.} *

