1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of our second weapon against the Classical Caro. Here we will analyze the historic Capablanca Variation (4...Bf5), which long served as the main line before the discoveries hidden in the Tartakower (4...Nf6). I’ve used this variation extensively over the years and am excited to share its ideas and nuances with you. The game begins with 1.e4. We reply with 1...c6, preparing the Caro pawn chain.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White classically controls the center with 2.d4. As always, we challenge it directly with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, entering the Classical Variation. Our solution is to capture on e4, ceding some central space in exchange for smooth development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. As mentioned in the introduction, instead of playing the Tartakower, we choose 4...Bf5, entering the Capablanca Variation. In this line, we aim to develop our pieces efficiently and without concessions.} 4...Bf5 5.Ng3 {Retreating the knight to g3 with tempo is White’s best and most popular option, played a massive 76% of the time. We simply retreat our bishop to g6, keeping it active on a strong diagonal.} 5...Bg6 6.f4 {White chooses the aggressive 6.f4, an option played only 3% of the time, but nothing to worry about. We reply with the simple 6...e6, an easy way to prevent f5.} 6...e6 7.Nf3 {White continues developing with 7.Nf3.} 7...Bd6 {We follow suit with 7...Bd6. We plan to bring our knights out via d7 and e7, with ...Ne7 ensuring White can’t push f5 at all. We remain very solid with excellent light-square control and smooth development.} *

