1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of our second weapon against the Classical Caro. Here we will analyze the historic Capablanca Variation (4...Bf5), which long served as the main line before the discoveries hidden in the Tartakower (4...Nf6). I’ve used this variation extensively over the years and am excited to share its ideas and nuances with you. The game begins with 1.e4. We reply with 1...c6, preparing the Caro pawn chain.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White classically controls the center with 2.d4. As always, we challenge it directly with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, entering the Classical Variation. Our solution is to capture on e4, ceding some central space in exchange for smooth development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. As mentioned in the introduction, instead of playing the Tartakower, we choose 4...Bf5, entering the Capablanca Variation. In this line, we aim to develop our pieces efficiently and without concessions.} 4...Bf5 5.Nc3 {White can also retreat to c3, a safe move but not particularly impressive. This option is played only 4% of the time.} 5...Nf6 {We continue with simple development, 5...Nf6. Interestingly, we’ve already examined this position in the Tartakower via 3...Nf6 4.Nc3 Bf5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Classical Variation (4...Bf5) / Study 1: Deviations to the Main Line Part 1: Early Sidelines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/b1f9a133-3c74-4b87-b12e-7c906e233da6/studies/60e54525-56c7-4126-8191-16550f8c28aa/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of our second weapon against the Classical Caro. Here we will analyze the historic Capablanca Variation (4...Bf5), which long served as the main line before the discoveries hidden in the Tartakower (4...Nf6). I’ve used this variation extensively over the years and am excited to share its ideas and nuances with you. The game begins with 1.e4. We reply with 1...c6, preparing the Caro pawn chain.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White classically controls the center with 2.d4. As always, we challenge it directly with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, entering the Classical Variation. Our solution is to capture on e4, ceding some central space in exchange for smooth development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. As mentioned in the introduction, instead of playing the Tartakower, we choose 4...Bf5, entering the Capablanca Variation. In this line, we aim to develop our pieces efficiently and without concessions.} 4...Bf5 5.Bd3 {White chooses to protect the knight with 5.Bd3, but this comes at the cost of the d4 pawn, meaning White is either gambiting or blundering. This is actually the second most popular move, played 10% of the time. We accept the gift and capture on d4 with the queen; 5...e6 is also an option for the risk-averse.} 5...Qxd4 6.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, gaining a tempo on our queen. We safely retreat the queen to its home square, also keeping the option to trade on e4 or d1 and enjoy our extra pawn.} 6...Qd8 7.Qe2 {White steps out of the trade with 7.Qe2, lining the queen up with our king.} 7...e6 {We respond with 7...e6, preparing to trade on e4 and develop solidly with Nd7 or Nf6. We must develop quickly before White generates meaningful threats. Another option was 7...Nf6!?, which looks like a blunder but isn’t thanks to the Qa5+ fork defending Bf5 - an idea we examined via transposition in the Tartakower sidelines study.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Classical Variation (4...Bf5) / Study 2: Deviations to the Main Line Part 2: White's Nf4 Ideas / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/b1f9a133-3c74-4b87-b12e-7c906e233da6/studies/7b76e984-7c73-4f96-a54a-f997a53185a6/lines"]

1.e4 {Our previous study covered early sidelines and deviations from White. Now we go deeper into aggressive ideas that often involve Nf4, reached via h3 or e2. White begins by taking the center with 1.e4. As always, we reply 1...c6, entering the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs maximum central control with 2.d4. We contest the center with 2...d5, forming our typical light-square Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White chooses the Classical Caro with 3.Nc3, defending the center while developing. We take on e4, opening the position so our pieces can activate quickly.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures with the knight. After 4...Bf5, we enter the Capablanca Caro, gaining a tempo on the knight with a developing bishop move.} 4...Bf5 5.Ng3 {The knight retreats to g3 with tempo on our bishop. We slide the bishop back to g6 for safety. From here, we focus on White’s aggressive Nf4 ideas, which can arise through Nh3, Ne2, h4, or even Bc4. Many of these move orders transpose, but you’ll be fully equipped to defend the Caro’s honor.} 5...Bg6 6.Bc4 {Bringing the bishop out to c4 is active and flexible. 6.Bc4 is played only 3% of the time, yet it’s the third most popular move. We blunt the bishop with 6...e6, also preparing development.} 6...e6 7.N1e2 {White plays 7.N1e2, developing and hinting at Nf4. We answer with 7...Bd6, a flexible move that eyes the f4 square while delaying ...Nf6 for extra flexibility.} 7...Bd6 8.O-O {White castles short, a flexible move that delays Nf4. We respond with 8...Nd7, once again keeping the kingside knight on g8 a bit longer.} 8...Nd7 9.f4 {9.f4 is White’s surprising idea, switching from Nf4 to f4 instead. If 9.Nf4, we can try our fancy 9...Qh4!? again or the simpler ...Ngf6.} 9...Ne7 {We continue with 9...Ne7! The whole point of delaying ...Ngf6 is revealed: we now control the light squares and completely prevent f4–f5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Classical Variation (4...Bf5) / Study 2: Deviations to the Main Line Part 2: White's Nf4 Ideas / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/b1f9a133-3c74-4b87-b12e-7c906e233da6/studies/7b76e984-7c73-4f96-a54a-f997a53185a6/lines"]

1.e4 {Our previous study covered early sidelines and deviations from White. Now we go deeper into aggressive ideas that often involve Nf4, reached via h3 or e2. White begins by taking the center with 1.e4. As always, we reply 1...c6, entering the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs maximum central control with 2.d4. We contest the center with 2...d5, forming our typical light-square Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White chooses the Classical Caro with 3.Nc3, defending the center while developing. We take on e4, opening the position so our pieces can activate quickly.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures with the knight. After 4...Bf5, we enter the Capablanca Caro, gaining a tempo on the knight with a developing bishop move.} 4...Bf5 5.Ng3 {The knight retreats to g3 with tempo on our bishop. We slide the bishop back to g6 for safety. From here, we focus on White’s aggressive Nf4 ideas, which can arise through Nh3, Ne2, h4, or even Bc4. Many of these move orders transpose, but you’ll be fully equipped to defend the Caro’s honor.} 5...Bg6 6.h4 {Our main line in this study begins with 6.h4, pushing the h-pawn to trap our bishop. This move is played 32% of the time and is the second most popular. We respond with 6...h6, creating an escape square.} 6...h6 7.Nh3 {In this study we examine 7.Nh3, a dangerous alternative to the main move 7.Nf3, aiming to attack the bishop via f4. We continue with the typical Caro push 7...e6, preparing development.} 7...e6 8.Nf4 {White completes the knight maneuver with 8.Nf4, attacking our bishop. We calmly retreat to h7, avoiding doubled pawns and preserving our good bishop.} 8...Bh7 9.Bc4 {White develops the bishop to c4, the most aggressive approach, trying to make sacrifices on e6 work. As long as we develop accurately, we have nothing to fear. We meet the pressure with 9...Nf6, while White may aim at e6 with the queen or rook.} 9...Nf6 10.O-O {White castles short, preparing Re1. We answer with 10...Bd6, adding pressure to the knight on f4.} 10...Bd6 11.Nxe6 {White goes for a famous Tal-style sacrifice, but it simply doesn’t work. We accept the free piece by capturing the knight.} 11...fxe6 12.Bxe6 {White takes on e6 with the bishop, gaining two pawns for the knight. Our king remains in the center, so White has some compensation, but we can defend. We continue with calm development, 12...Nbd7.} 12...Nbd7 13.Re1 {White places the rook on the open e-file, setting up potential discoveries.} 13...Qc7 {We don’t fear any ghost checks and play 13...Qc7, leaving White without meaningful discovered attacks. Our plan is to castle long, forcing White to give a check with the bishop to stop it. With all our pieces developed, our extra piece comfortably outweighs White’s initiative. With solid defense, you will convert the win.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Classical Variation (4...Bf5) / Study 2: Deviations to the Main Line Part 2: White's Nf4 Ideas / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/b1f9a133-3c74-4b87-b12e-7c906e233da6/studies/7b76e984-7c73-4f96-a54a-f997a53185a6/lines"]

1.e4 {Our previous study covered early sidelines and deviations from White. Now we go deeper into aggressive ideas that often involve Nf4, reached via h3 or e2. White begins by taking the center with 1.e4. As always, we reply 1...c6, entering the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs maximum central control with 2.d4. We contest the center with 2...d5, forming our typical light-square Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White chooses the Classical Caro with 3.Nc3, defending the center while developing. We take on e4, opening the position so our pieces can activate quickly.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures with the knight. After 4...Bf5, we enter the Capablanca Caro, gaining a tempo on the knight with a developing bishop move.} 4...Bf5 5.Ng3 {The knight retreats to g3 with tempo on our bishop. We slide the bishop back to g6 for safety. From here, we focus on White’s aggressive Nf4 ideas, which can arise through Nh3, Ne2, h4, or even Bc4. Many of these move orders transpose, but you’ll be fully equipped to defend the Caro’s honor.} 5...Bg6 6.Nh3 {White plays 6.Nh3, aiming for Nf4 while keeping Bc4 ideas in reserve. This is an instructive plan but played only 2% of the time. We answer with 6...e6, always choosing ...e6 before ...Nf6 to keep our flexibility.} 6...e6 7.Nf4 {White carries out the main idea with 7.Nf4; instead, 7.Bc4 Bd6 8.Nf4 Qh4!? is our quirky line to attack f4 and prevent h4. Here we immediately respond with 7...Qh4, disrupting White’s h4 plans and pressuring the fourth rank. Another idea was 7...Bd6 or ...Qc7 to target the Nf4.} 7...Qh4 8.c3 {White protects the d4-pawn. We simply develop with 8...Nd7, as if nothing happened.} 8...Nd7 9.Qf3 {White improves the queen to f3 to support the knight. We continue developing by bringing our king's knight out to f6.} 9...Ngf6 10.Nxg6 {White eventually decides to take our bishop with 10.Nxg6.} 10...hxg6 {We recapture the piece, keeping our structure solid. With our development secure, a key idea here is the e5 break to activate our pieces further.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Classical Variation (4...Bf5) / Study 2: Deviations to the Main Line Part 2: White's Nf4 Ideas / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/b1f9a133-3c74-4b87-b12e-7c906e233da6/studies/7b76e984-7c73-4f96-a54a-f997a53185a6/lines"]

1.e4 {Our previous study covered early sidelines and deviations from White. Now we go deeper into aggressive ideas that often involve Nf4, reached via h3 or e2. White begins by taking the center with 1.e4. As always, we reply 1...c6, entering the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs maximum central control with 2.d4. We contest the center with 2...d5, forming our typical light-square Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White chooses the Classical Caro with 3.Nc3, defending the center while developing. We take on e4, opening the position so our pieces can activate quickly.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures with the knight. After 4...Bf5, we enter the Capablanca Caro, gaining a tempo on the knight with a developing bishop move.} 4...Bf5 5.Ng3 {The knight retreats to g3 with tempo on our bishop. We slide the bishop back to g6 for safety. From here, we focus on White’s aggressive Nf4 ideas, which can arise through Nh3, Ne2, h4, or even Bc4. Many of these move orders transpose, but you’ll be fully equipped to defend the Caro’s honor.} 5...Bg6 6.h4 {Our main line in this study begins with 6.h4, pushing the h-pawn to trap our bishop. This move is played 32% of the time and is the second most popular. We respond with 6...h6, creating an escape square.} 6...h6 7.Nh3 {In this study we examine 7.Nh3, a dangerous alternative to the main move 7.Nf3, aiming to attack the bishop via f4. We continue with the typical Caro push 7...e6, preparing development.} 7...e6 8.Nf4 {White completes the knight maneuver with 8.Nf4, attacking our bishop. We calmly retreat to h7, avoiding doubled pawns and preserving our good bishop.} 8...Bh7 9.Bc4 {White develops the bishop to c4, the most aggressive approach, trying to make sacrifices on e6 work. As long as we develop accurately, we have nothing to fear. We meet the pressure with 9...Nf6, while White may aim at e6 with the queen or rook.} 9...Nf6 10.O-O {White castles short, preparing Re1. We answer with 10...Bd6, adding pressure to the knight on f4.} 10...Bd6 11.Re1 {11.Re1 is a calmer but stronger move, lining the rook up with our king. We are now just in time to castle short, despite all of White’s pressure on e6.} 11...O-O 12.c3 {The game may continue with White reinforcing the center.} 12...Re8 {We end the variation with 12...Re8, safeguarding e6. ...Nd7 and ...Qc7 are common follow-ups, and the e5 break is on the agenda.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Classical Variation (4...Bf5) / Study 2: Deviations to the Main Line Part 2: White's Nf4 Ideas / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/b1f9a133-3c74-4b87-b12e-7c906e233da6/studies/7b76e984-7c73-4f96-a54a-f997a53185a6/lines"]

1.e4 {Our previous study covered early sidelines and deviations from White. Now we go deeper into aggressive ideas that often involve Nf4, reached via h3 or e2. White begins by taking the center with 1.e4. As always, we reply 1...c6, entering the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs maximum central control with 2.d4. We contest the center with 2...d5, forming our typical light-square Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White chooses the Classical Caro with 3.Nc3, defending the center while developing. We take on e4, opening the position so our pieces can activate quickly.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures with the knight. After 4...Bf5, we enter the Capablanca Caro, gaining a tempo on the knight with a developing bishop move.} 4...Bf5 5.Ng3 {The knight retreats to g3 with tempo on our bishop. We slide the bishop back to g6 for safety. From here, we focus on White’s aggressive Nf4 ideas, which can arise through Nh3, Ne2, h4, or even Bc4. Many of these move orders transpose, but you’ll be fully equipped to defend the Caro’s honor.} 5...Bg6 6.Bc4 {Bringing the bishop out to c4 is active and flexible. 6.Bc4 is played only 3% of the time, yet it’s the third most popular move. We blunt the bishop with 6...e6, also preparing development.} 6...e6 7.N1e2 {White plays 7.N1e2, developing and hinting at Nf4. We answer with 7...Bd6, a flexible move that eyes the f4 square while delaying ...Nf6 for extra flexibility.} 7...Bd6 8.h4 {The aggressive 8.h4 has scored well for White. We respond by creating a retreat square with 8...h6.} 8...h6 9.Nf4 {White executes the knight jump to f4. We take on f4, expediting development at the cost of the bishops. However, White’s king lacks a safe home, with the kingside weakened by the h-pawn and the queenside facing a potential pawn storm and the bishop on g6.} 9...Bxf4 10.Bxf4 {White recaptures the piece.} 10...Nf6 {We end the variation by completing our kingside development. White’s king will be under pressure on either side of the board since we kept the g6-bishop alive.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Classical Variation (4...Bf5) / Study 2: Deviations to the Main Line Part 2: White's Nf4 Ideas / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/b1f9a133-3c74-4b87-b12e-7c906e233da6/studies/7b76e984-7c73-4f96-a54a-f997a53185a6/lines"]

1.e4 {Our previous study covered early sidelines and deviations from White. Now we go deeper into aggressive ideas that often involve Nf4, reached via h3 or e2. White begins by taking the center with 1.e4. As always, we reply 1...c6, entering the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs maximum central control with 2.d4. We contest the center with 2...d5, forming our typical light-square Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White chooses the Classical Caro with 3.Nc3, defending the center while developing. We take on e4, opening the position so our pieces can activate quickly.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures with the knight. After 4...Bf5, we enter the Capablanca Caro, gaining a tempo on the knight with a developing bishop move.} 4...Bf5 5.Ng3 {The knight retreats to g3 with tempo on our bishop. We slide the bishop back to g6 for safety. From here, we focus on White’s aggressive Nf4 ideas, which can arise through Nh3, Ne2, h4, or even Bc4. Many of these move orders transpose, but you’ll be fully equipped to defend the Caro’s honor.} 5...Bg6 6.Bc4 {Bringing the bishop out to c4 is active and flexible. 6.Bc4 is played only 3% of the time, yet it’s the third most popular move. We blunt the bishop with 6...e6, also preparing development.} 6...e6 7.N1e2 {White plays 7.N1e2, developing and hinting at Nf4. We answer with 7...Bd6, a flexible move that eyes the f4 square while delaying ...Nf6 for extra flexibility.} 7...Bd6 8.Nf4 {White continues with the main idea, 8.Nf4.} 8...Qh4 {Our solution is 8...Qh4!, an unusual but thematic move in this line. The queen not only targets the f4-knight but also prevents h4–h5 from chasing our bishop. We even encourage White to capture on g6 so that after hxg6 the h-file opens. ...Nf6, ...Nd7, and ...0-0-0 are natural follow-ups.} *

