with 12...Be7, a revolutionary idea in the Caro: Black is going to play actively and castle short. The older approach with 12...Qc7 followed by 0-0-0 gave the Caro a solid but dull reputation. The discovery that castling short is sound sparked huge interest, attracting even attacking players to this variation.} 12...Be7 13.Qe2 {13.Qe2 is an interesting retreat. White makes room for the d1-rook to gain influence. We respond immediately with 13...c5!, striking the center and, more importantly, opening the c-file for future counterplay.} 13...c5 14.dxc5 {White captures on c5, exchanging in the center.} 14...Qc7 {We don’t recapture on c5 right away and instead play 14...Qc7, a clean sidestep out of the Rd1 line. Now we’ll be ready to castle and recapture on c5 with strong counterplay.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Variation (3...dxe4) / Study 1: The Tartakower Transposition  3..dxe4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/883d554f-1bdb-4865-bcf4-4feafd5c751c/studies/9b988a93-1b7d-4bf6-98be-9720e0148b25/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Two Knights Caro chapter. The Two Knights is a very sound and solid system for White, quickly bringing out two knights as the name suggests. In this study we’ll examine a possible transposition into the Tartakower (if that’s your weapon against the Classical). The game begins with 1.e4, placing a pawn in the center. As always we reply with 1...c6, preparing the Caro counter.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight with 2.Nc3, controlling the center. For the purposes of the Two Knights, 2.Nf3 and 2.Nc3 are interchangeable, as both moves transpose. We continue by building the solid Caro pawn chain with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White plays 3.Nf3, officially entering the Two Knights Caro, using both knights to control the center. As noted in the introduction, we capture on e4 with 3...dxe4, aiming to steer the game into Tartakower territory.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4 with the knight. We continue with another typical Tartakower move, 4...Nf6, offering the knight trade and not worrying about doubled f-pawns.} 4...Nf6 5.Neg5 {Declining the trade with 5.Neg5 is not a good move, but some tricky players may go for a quick attack, which simply doesn’t work. Still, about 4% of players will try it. We immediately respond with 5...h6, kicking the knight away.} 5...h6 6.Nxf7 {White’s whole idea is to sacrifice on f7, but there’s nowhere near enough support for the attack. We take the knight for free.} 6...Kxf7 7.Bc4+ {White continues with the ambitious 7.Bc4+, developing with check. We calmly block with 7...e6.} 7...e6 8.Ne5+ {White keeps up the aggressive style with 8.Ne5+, checking our king again. We retreat to g8, where the king is perfectly safe.} 8...Kg8 9.d4 {With no immediate threats left, White prepares to bring more pieces into the game by occupying the center with 9.d4. We counter the center and prepare development with 9...c5.} 9...c5 10.c3 {White reinforces the center with 10.c3. We strike back with 10...Nc6, targeting the center and offering trades.} 10...Nc6 11.Ng6 {White avoids the trade and attacks our rook with 11.Ng6.} 11...cxd4 {We can simply give up the rook for the knight - we’re up so much material that it doesn’t matter. We take in the center with 11...cxd4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Variation (3...dxe4) / Study 1: The Tartakower Transposition  3..dxe4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/883d554f-1bdb-4865-bcf4-4feafd5c751c/studies/9b988a93-1b7d-4bf6-98be-9720e0148b25/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Two Knights Caro chapter. The Two Knights is a very sound and solid system for White, quickly bringing out two knights as the name suggests. In this study we’ll examine a possible transposition into the Tartakower (if that’s your weapon against the Classical). The game begins with 1.e4, placing a pawn in the center. As always we reply with 1...c6, preparing the Caro counter.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight with 2.Nc3, controlling the center. For the purposes of the Two Knights, 2.Nf3 and 2.Nc3 are interchangeable, as both moves transpose. We continue by building the solid Caro pawn chain with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White plays 3.Nf3, officially entering the Two Knights Caro, using both knights to control the center. As noted in the introduction, we capture on e4 with 3...dxe4, aiming to steer the game into Tartakower territory.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4 with the knight. We continue with another typical Tartakower move, 4...Nf6, offering the knight trade and not worrying about doubled f-pawns.} 4...Nf6 5.Ng3 {Declining the knight trade and retreating to g3 is a passive but solid option, White chooses it about 20% of the time. We reply with the interesting idea 5...c5, preventing White from playing d4. Next we’ll develop with ...e6, ...Nc6, ...Be7, ...0-0, and even ...b6–Bb7.} 5...c5 6.Bc4 {White may continue with normal development via 6.Bc4, aiming at f7 and making it harder for us to develop the light-squared bishop to g4. We simply keep developing with 6...Nc6, controlling the d4 square.} 6...Nc6 7.O-O {White castles. We consolidate our structure with 7...e6, keeping the light-squared bishop inside the pawn chain for now.} 7...e6 8.Re1 {White places the rook on the semi-open e-file. We continue developing with 8...Be7, preparing to castle.} 8...Be7 9.d3 {White plays 9.d3, both sides are simply completing development at this point. Note that White could have played d3 earlier, since the idea is purely developmental. We now castle.} 9...O-O 10.Bf4 {White develops the last minor piece, bringing the bishop out to f4.} 10...b6 {Since we played 7...e6 with our light-squared bishop still inside the pawn chain, we look to fianchetto it with 10...b6.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Variation (3...dxe4) / Study 1: The Tartakower Transposition  3..dxe4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/883d554f-1bdb-4865-bcf4-4feafd5c751c/studies/9b988a93-1b7d-4bf6-98be-9720e0148b25/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Two Knights Caro chapter. The Two Knights is a very sound and solid system for White, quickly bringing out two knights as the name suggests. In this study we’ll examine a possible transposition into the Tartakower (if that’s your weapon against the Classical). The game begins with 1.e4, placing a pawn in the center. As always we reply with 1...c6, preparing the Caro counter.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight with 2.Nc3, controlling the center. For the purposes of the Two Knights, 2.Nf3 and 2.Nc3 are interchangeable, as both moves transpose. We continue by building the solid Caro pawn chain with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White plays 3.Nf3, officially entering the Two Knights Caro, using both knights to control the center. As noted in the introduction, we capture on e4 with 3...dxe4, aiming to steer the game into Tartakower territory.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4 with the knight. We continue with another typical Tartakower move, 4...Nf6, offering the knight trade and not worrying about doubled f-pawns.} 4...Nf6 5.Qe2 {White’s main line is the tricky 5.Qe2, which keeps the game in true Two Knights spirit. This move is fairly common, played in about 19% of games, and we need to know how to counter it. Our solution is to take on e4, removing the central tension; just don’t blunder with something like 5...Nbd7?? allowing 6.Nd6 mate.} 5...Nxe4 6.Qxe4 {White is forced to recapture with the queen. Here we have an interesting idea: 6...Qa5, preparing ...Bf5 while also preventing d4 by pinning the pawn to the king.} 6...Qa5 7.Bc4 {White often continues with the logical developing move 7.Bc4. As planned, we play 7...Bf5, developing with tempo on the queen.} 7...Bf5 8.Qe2 {White can also opt for a safe and solid retreat with 8.Qe2. We continue with normal development by playing 8...Nd7.} 8...Nd7 9.O-O {White castles short. We respond with a typical Caro move, 9...e6, preparing to finish our kingside development.} 9...e6 10.d4 {White takes the center with 10.d4. We calmly continue developing with 10...Be7, reaching a very solid position.} 10...Be7 11.c3 {White builds a sturdy pawn chain with 11.c3. Both sides are solid and developing naturally, so the position is roughly equal.} 11...O-O {We finish by castling, completing development. From here, we can consider queenside play with ...b5 or ...a5, and we’ll also place our rooks on the e- and d-files.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Variation (3...dxe4) / Study 1: The Tartakower Transposition  3..dxe4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/883d554f-1bdb-4865-bcf4-4feafd5c751c/studies/9b988a93-1b7d-4bf6-98be-9720e0148b25/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Two Knights Caro chapter. The Two Knights is a very sound and solid system for White, quickly bringing out two knights as the name suggests. In this study we’ll examine a possible transposition into the Tartakower (if that’s your weapon against the Classical). The game begins with 1.e4, placing a pawn in the center. As always we reply with 1...c6, preparing the Caro counter.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight with 2.Nc3, controlling the center. For the purposes of the Two Knights, 2.Nf3 and 2.Nc3 are interchangeable, as both moves transpose. We continue by building the solid Caro pawn chain with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White plays 3.Nf3, officially entering the Two Knights Caro, using both knights to control the center. As noted in the introduction, we capture on e4 with 3...dxe4, aiming to steer the game into Tartakower territory.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4 with the knight. We continue with another typical Tartakower move, 4...Nf6, offering the knight trade and not worrying about doubled f-pawns.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White also has the option to capture on f6, but this is exactly what we want! It’s the most common move, played in about 46% of games. We recapture with the e-pawn, and now the position strongly resembles a Tartakower. In fact, after White’s natural 6.d4, the game transposes.} 5...exf6 6.Qe2+ {White tries to take control of the open e-file early with 6.Qe2+, but this check doesn’t bother us at all. We simply develop our dark-squared bishop to e7, blocking the check.} 6...Be7 7.d4 {White now decides to seize the center with 7.d4. It’s time for us to get our king to safety.} 7...O-O 8.Be3 {With 8.Be3, White prepares to castle long. We answer by sliding the rook to the open e-file, lining it up against the enemy queen and king.} 8...Re8 9.O-O-O {White castles long. We continue developing with 9...Be6, angling our bishops toward the queenside.} 9...Be6 10.Kb1 {White defends the a2 pawn by sliding the king to b1.} 10...b5 {With opposite-side castling on the board, we go for 10...b5, not only preparing a pawn storm but also opening the door for ...Qd5, which directly threatens the king.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Variation (3...dxe4) / Study 1: The Tartakower Transposition  3..dxe4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/883d554f-1bdb-4865-bcf4-4feafd5c751c/studies/9b988a93-1b7d-4bf6-98be-9720e0148b25/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Two Knights Caro chapter. The Two Knights is a very sound and solid system for White, quickly bringing out two knights as the name suggests. In this study we’ll examine a possible transposition into the Tartakower (if that’s your weapon against the Classical). The game begins with 1.e4, placing a pawn in the center. As always we reply with 1...c6, preparing the Caro counter.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight with 2.Nc3, controlling the center. For the purposes of the Two Knights, 2.Nf3 and 2.Nc3 are interchangeable, as both moves transpose. We continue by building the solid Caro pawn chain with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White plays 3.Nf3, officially entering the Two Knights Caro, using both knights to control the center. As noted in the introduction, we capture on e4 with 3...dxe4, aiming to steer the game into Tartakower territory.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4 with the knight. We continue with another typical Tartakower move, 4...Nf6, offering the knight trade and not worrying about doubled f-pawns.} 4...Nf6 5.Qe2 {White’s main line is the tricky 5.Qe2, which keeps the game in true Two Knights spirit. This move is fairly common, played in about 19% of games, and we need to know how to counter it. Our solution is to take on e4, removing the central tension; just don’t blunder with something like 5...Nbd7?? allowing 6.Nd6 mate.} 5...Nxe4 6.Qxe4 {White is forced to recapture with the queen. Here we have an interesting idea: 6...Qa5, preparing ...Bf5 while also preventing d4 by pinning the pawn to the king.} 6...Qa5 7.Bc4 {White often continues with the logical developing move 7.Bc4. As planned, we play 7...Bf5, developing with tempo on the queen.} 7...Bf5 8.Qf4 {White’s most common option is to keep the queen active by sliding it to f4. We continue with 8...e6, preparing further development and keeping a very solid position. We’re completely comfortable after our pieces come out via e7 and d7, followed by castling. Be cautious about grabbing the c2 pawn if White offers it, d3! can strand our bishop.} 8...e6 9.O-O {White castles. We prepare to castle as well by developing our kingside with 9...Be7.} 9...Be7 10.c3 {A typical continuation for White is 10.c3, preparing d4. We simply get to safety and prepare to bring our knight out next.} 10...O-O 11.d4 {White establishes a central pawn with 11.d4.} 11...Nd7 {We complete development with 11...Nd7, reaching a very comfortable position to end the variation.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Variation (3...dxe4) / Study 1: The Tartakower Transposition  3..dxe4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/883d554f-1bdb-4865-bcf4-4feafd5c751c/studies/9b988a93-1b7d-4bf6-98be-9720e0148b25/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Two Knights Caro chapter. The Two Knights is a very sound and solid system for White, quickly bringing out two knights as the name suggests. In this study we’ll examine a possible transposition into the Tartakower (if that’s your weapon against the Classical). The game begins with 1.e4, placing a pawn in the center. As always we reply with 1...c6, preparing the Caro counter.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight with 2.Nc3, controlling the center. For the purposes of the Two Knights, 2.Nf3 and 2.Nc3 are interchangeable, as both moves transpose. We continue by building the solid Caro pawn chain with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White plays 3.Nf3, officially entering the Two Knights Caro, using both knights to control the center. As noted in the introduction, we capture on e4 with 3...dxe4, aiming to steer the game into Tartakower territory.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4 with the knight. We continue with another typical Tartakower move, 4...Nf6, offering the knight trade and not worrying about doubled f-pawns.} 4...Nf6 5.Qe2 {White’s main line is the tricky 5.Qe2, which keeps the game in true Two Knights spirit. This move is fairly common, played in about 19% of games, and we need to know how to counter it. Our solution is to take on e4, removing the central tension; just don’t blunder with something like 5...Nbd7?? allowing 6.Nd6 mate.} 5...Nxe4 6.Qxe4 {White is forced to recapture with the queen. Here we have an interesting idea: 6...Qa5, preparing ...Bf5 while also preventing d4 by pinning the pawn to the king.} 6...Qa5 7.b4 {An interesting and active solution for White is 7.b4, aiming to chase our queen.} 7...Qd5 {We escape the danger by sliding to d5, offering a queen trade while still keeping ...Bf5 in reserve if the queen moves away from e4.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Fantasy Variation (3...e5) / Study 1: The Wild 3...e5 Countergambit / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/a78836b0-79c4-483a-9b2a-bac178d52a1d/studies/660a9eba-2cf9-4fbc-950c-74e0831d221f/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we deal with the Fantasy Variation, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro–Kann. I want to show you the 3...e5 weapon against it. White begins by occupying the center, and we respond in true Caro style.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs space in the center with 2.d4. We counter with 2...d5, building our Caro pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White plays 3.f3, a funny-looking idea, but that’s the Fantasy! White refuses to give up the e4–d4 duo, even using the f-pawn to support it. We respond sharply with 3...e5!, aiming to eliminate the d4 pawn and exploit the weakened g1–a7 diagonal.} 3...e5 4.dxe5 {White accepts our gambit 62% of the time - it makes sense to take a free pawn in the center! We immediately seize control of the weakened diagonal with 4...Bc5.} 4...Bc5 5.Bd3 {A reasonable developing move, chosen about 6% of the time. We immediately strike along the open diagonal and play 5...Qb6.} 5...Qb6 6.Ne2 {White sidesteps our ...Bxg1 idea and develops the attacked knight to e2. We also bring our knight out, 6...Nd7, developing with tempo.} 6...Nd7 7.f4 {White defends e5 with their f-pawn. Our response is to open the center with 7...dxe4.} 7...dxe4 8.Bxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. We undermine e5 with 8...f6. If White captures on f6, then after ...Nfxf6, our pieces flood the board.} 8...f6 9.e6 {Pushing the e-pawn to attack the knight looks tempting but loses. It's time for us to check the king with our bishop, 9...Bf2+, clearing the c5 square for our knight.} 9...Bf2+ 10.Kf1 {White's king is forced to f1 since 10. Kd2 Qe3 is mate!} 10...Nc5 {We escape danger by playing 10...Nc5. We will get our pawn back with massive development and a winning position.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Fantasy Variation (3...e5) / Study 1: The Wild 3...e5 Countergambit / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/a78836b0-79c4-483a-9b2a-bac178d52a1d/studies/660a9eba-2cf9-4fbc-950c-74e0831d221f/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we deal with the Fantasy Variation, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro–Kann. I want to show you the 3...e5 weapon against it. White begins by occupying the center, and we respond in true Caro style.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs space in the center with 2.d4. We counter with 2...d5, building our Caro pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White plays 3.f3, a funny-looking idea, but that’s the Fantasy! White refuses to give up the e4–d4 duo, even using the f-pawn to support it. We respond sharply with 3...e5!, aiming to eliminate the d4 pawn and exploit the weakened g1–a7 diagonal.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops and supports the center, a natural move, played about 10% of the time. The simplest solution for us is ...dxe4.} 4...dxe4 5.dxe5 {White takes on e5, making the queens stare at each other. We respond with a queen trade.} 5...Qxd1+ 6.Nxd1 {White recaptures with the knight to keep the option of castling. We develop with 6...Nd7, attacking e5. There’s no need to play ...exf3 and help White develop.} 6...Nd7 7.f4 {White supports e5 with their f-pawn. We immediately undermine the structure by playing 7...f6. White’s position is under fire; if Nf2, we play ...fxe5 and enjoy superior development.} 7...f6 8.exf6 {Most players decide to capture, which only speeds up our development. We bring out the knight, developing and recapturing.} 8...Ngxf6 9.Nc3 {White can continue developing, trying to come back into the game.} 9...Bc5 {After 9...Bc5 we already have a better position because of the edge in development.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Fantasy Variation (3...e5) / Study 1: The Wild 3...e5 Countergambit / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/a78836b0-79c4-483a-9b2a-bac178d52a1d/studies/660a9eba-2cf9-4fbc-950c-74e0831d221f/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we deal with the Fantasy Variation, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro–Kann. I want to show you the 3...e5 weapon against it. White begins by occupying the center, and we respond in true Caro style.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs space in the center with 2.d4. We counter with 2...d5, building our Caro pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White plays 3.f3, a funny-looking idea, but that’s the Fantasy! White refuses to give up the e4–d4 duo, even using the f-pawn to support it. We respond sharply with 3...e5!, aiming to eliminate the d4 pawn and exploit the weakened g1–a7 diagonal.} 3...e5 4.dxe5 {White accepts our gambit 62% of the time - it makes sense to take a free pawn in the center! We immediately seize control of the weakened diagonal with 4...Bc5.} 4...Bc5 5.Nc3 {White’s best response is to keep developing with 5.Nc3, often followed by Na4 ideas. You’ll see this in about 38% of games. We continue developing normally with 5...Ne7.} 5...Ne7 6.exd5 {White’s next move is the most popular choice, but it’s questionable since opening the position only helps us. Expect 6.exd5 in roughly 34% of games. We recapture the pawn, opening lines for our pieces.} 6...cxd5 7.Bb5+ {Checking our king with the bishop is tempting but only accelerates our development. We respond by blocking the check with our undeveloped knight. We’re still down a pawn, but our activity and safer king give us full compensation.} 7...Nbc6 8.Nge2 {White develops the last kingside minor piece but still can’t castle because of our dark-squared bishop. We castle ourselves, bringing the king to safety while also threatening ...Nxe5.} 8...O-O 9.f4 {White focuses on saving the pawn instead of developing.} 9...f6 {We undermine e5 with 9...f6, activating the rook and opening lines toward White’s king. Our bishop dominates from c5, and soon the queen and light-squared bishop will join the attack from b6 and g4.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Fantasy Variation (3...e5) / Study 1: The Wild 3...e5 Countergambit / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/a78836b0-79c4-483a-9b2a-bac178d52a1d/studies/660a9eba-2cf9-4fbc-950c-74e0831d221f/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we deal with the Fantasy Variation, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro–Kann. I want to show you the 3...e5 weapon against it. White begins by occupying the center, and we respond in true Caro style.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs space in the center with 2.d4. We counter with 2...d5, building our Caro pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White plays 3.f3, a funny-looking idea, but that’s the Fantasy! White refuses to give up the e4–d4 duo, even using the f-pawn to support it. We respond sharply with 3...e5!, aiming to eliminate the d4 pawn and exploit the weakened g1–a7 diagonal.} 3...e5 4.dxe5 {White accepts our gambit 62% of the time - it makes sense to take a free pawn in the center! We immediately seize control of the weakened diagonal with 4...Bc5.} 4...Bc5 5.Nc3 {White’s best response is to keep developing with 5.Nc3, often followed by Na4 ideas. You’ll see this in about 38% of games. We continue developing normally with 5...Ne7.} 5...Ne7 6.Bd3 {Developing the light-squared bishop to d3 is best, and it’s played about 13% of the time. We castle our king, a natural move. Though in practice Black has scored well with 6...b5!?.} 6...O-O 7.f4 {White plays 7.f4, preparing Nf3, a computer-approved response. Even if White manages to stabilize, the exposed king on e1 gives us long-term compensation. We develop creatively, bringing our knight out to a6.} 7...Na6 8.Nf3 {If White plays naturally with 8.Nf3 for example... We can attack the bishop on d3 by playing 8...Nb4.} 8...Nb4 9.Qe2 {White develops the queen to e2, continuing to defend the bishop.} 9...a5 {We’re in no rush to take the bishop, so 9...a5 is our move. If a3, we take on d3; if exd5, we recapture with ...Nexd5.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Fantasy Variation (3...e5) / Study 1: The Wild 3...e5 Countergambit / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/a78836b0-79c4-483a-9b2a-bac178d52a1d/studies/660a9eba-2cf9-4fbc-950c-74e0831d221f/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we deal with the Fantasy Variation, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro–Kann. I want to show you the 3...e5 weapon against it. White begins by occupying the center, and we respond in true Caro style.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs space in the center with 2.d4. We counter with 2...d5, building our Caro pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White plays 3.f3, a funny-looking idea, but that’s the Fantasy! White refuses to give up the e4–d4 duo, even using the f-pawn to support it. We respond sharply with 3...e5!, aiming to eliminate the d4 pawn and exploit the weakened g1–a7 diagonal.} 3...e5 4.dxe5 {White accepts our gambit 62% of the time - it makes sense to take a free pawn in the center! We immediately seize control of the weakened diagonal with 4...Bc5.} 4...Bc5 5.Ne2 {White develops with 5.Ne2, moving the knight out of our bishop’s gaze, but blocking the rest of their pieces. This is played about 8% of the time. We immediately go after the pawn and develop with 5...Nd7.} 5...Nd7 6.Bf4 {White plays 6.Bf4 defending e5 but leaving b2 loose.} 6...Qb6 {We end the variation with 6...Qb6, with a strong double attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Fantasy Variation (3...e5) / Study 1: The Wild 3...e5 Countergambit / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/a78836b0-79c4-483a-9b2a-bac178d52a1d/studies/660a9eba-2cf9-4fbc-950c-74e0831d221f/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we deal with the Fantasy Variation, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro–Kann. I want to show you the 3...e5 weapon against it. White begins by occupying the center, and we respond in true Caro style.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs space in the center with 2.d4. We counter with 2...d5, building our Caro pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White plays 3.f3, a funny-looking idea, but that’s the Fantasy! White refuses to give up the e4–d4 duo, even using the f-pawn to support it. We respond sharply with 3...e5!, aiming to eliminate the d4 pawn and exploit the weakened g1–a7 diagonal.} 3...e5 4.exd5 {White takes the wrong pawn 8% of the time. Our solution is to take on d4, luring out White's queen.} 4...exd4 5.Qxd4 {White recaptures the pawn back with the queen. It's time for us to take on d5 next. Our minor pieces will flood the center, and we’ll soon castle kingside.} 5...cxd5 6.Bb5+ {White can develop their light-squared bishop with check. We simply block with our knight.} 6...Nc6 7.Nc3 {White continues developing and plays 7.Nc3, attacking d5. We defend calmly with 7...Nf6.} 7...Nf6 8.Bg5 {White develops their dark-squared bishop and pins our newly developed knight, removing the defender of the d5 pawn. But we easily unpin, 8...Be7 is next.} 8...Be7 9.O-O-O {White castles queenside.} 9...O-O {And we castle kingside. Next, we'll activate our bishop to e6 to strengthen the center. The rook can occupy the semi-open c-file and the queen can activate to the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Fantasy Variation (3...e5) / Study 1: The Wild 3...e5 Countergambit / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/a78836b0-79c4-483a-9b2a-bac178d52a1d/studies/660a9eba-2cf9-4fbc-950c-74e0831d221f/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we deal with the Fantasy Variation, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro–Kann. I want to show you the 3...e5 weapon against it. White begins by occupying the center, and we respond in true Caro style.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs space in the center with 2.d4. We counter with 2...d5, building our Caro pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White plays 3.f3, a funny-looking idea, but that’s the Fantasy! White refuses to give up the e4–d4 duo, even using the f-pawn to support it. We respond sharply with 3...e5!, aiming to eliminate the d4 pawn and exploit the weakened g1–a7 diagonal.} 3...e5 4.dxe5 {White accepts our gambit 62% of the time - it makes sense to take a free pawn in the center! We immediately seize control of the weakened diagonal with 4...Bc5.} 4...Bc5 5.exd5 {Capturing on d5 is too greedy, although played 34% of the time. Our response is to strike on the weak diagonal with 5...Qb6, the whole point of our gambit!} 5...Qb6 6.Nh3 {White stops ...Bxg1 and ...Bf2+ simultaneously by playing 6.Nh3. But we can now take on h3, not only eliminating the defender of f2 but also ruining White's pawn structure.} 6...Bxh3 7.gxh3 {White recaptures the piece. We continue by taking on d5, with obvious compensation as we complete development and White's king stays stranded in the center.} 7...cxd5 8.Qxd5 {White greedily grabs a pawn with the queen. We can now take the chance to displace White's king. 8...Bf2+ is next.} 8...Bf2+ 9.Ke2 {White moves their king up since the d-file is too dangerous with a future ...Rd8. We continue with development, 9...Nc6, also setting up Rd8 ideas.} 9...Nc6 10.Nc3 {White also develops their knight, 10.Nc3.} 10...Bg1 {It's time for a sneaky intrusion! After 10...Bg1 we now we have serious threats of Qf2/Rd8.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Fantasy Variation (3...e5) / Study 1: The Wild 3...e5 Countergambit / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/a78836b0-79c4-483a-9b2a-bac178d52a1d/studies/660a9eba-2cf9-4fbc-950c-74e0831d221f/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we deal with the Fantasy Variation, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro–Kann. I want to show you the 3...e5 weapon against it. White begins by occupying the center, and we respond in true Caro style.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs space in the center with 2.d4. We counter with 2...d5, building our Caro pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White plays 3.f3, a funny-looking idea, but that’s the Fantasy! White refuses to give up the e4–d4 duo, even using the f-pawn to support it. We respond sharply with 3...e5!, aiming to eliminate the d4 pawn and exploit the weakened g1–a7 diagonal.} 3...e5 4.dxe5 {White accepts our gambit 62% of the time - it makes sense to take a free pawn in the center! We immediately seize control of the weakened diagonal with 4...Bc5.} 4...Bc5 5.Nc3 {White’s best response is to keep developing with 5.Nc3, often followed by Na4 ideas. You’ll see this in about 38% of games. We continue developing normally with 5...Ne7.} 5...Ne7 6.f4 {Playing 6.f4 is very ambitious, White wants to hold the material and develop with Nf3. It's played 5% of the time. We start generating threats right away with our thematic queen move, 6...Qb6.} 6...Qb6 7.Nf3 {White sidesteps the attack. This only forces their king into the open after 7...Bf2+.} 7...Bf2+ 8.Ke2 {White plays the only move that works.} 8...a5 {We end the variation with 8...a5, a clever idea, opening the a7 square for the queen in case of Na4.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Fantasy Variation (3...e5) / Study 1: The Wild 3...e5 Countergambit / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/a78836b0-79c4-483a-9b2a-bac178d52a1d/studies/660a9eba-2cf9-4fbc-950c-74e0831d221f/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we deal with the Fantasy Variation, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro–Kann. I want to show you the 3...e5 weapon against it. White begins by occupying the center, and we respond in true Caro style.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs space in the center with 2.d4. We counter with 2...d5, building our Caro pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White plays 3.f3, a funny-looking idea, but that’s the Fantasy! White refuses to give up the e4–d4 duo, even using the f-pawn to support it. We respond sharply with 3...e5!, aiming to eliminate the d4 pawn and exploit the weakened g1–a7 diagonal.} 3...e5 4.dxe5 {White accepts our gambit 62% of the time - it makes sense to take a free pawn in the center! We immediately seize control of the weakened diagonal with 4...Bc5.} 4...Bc5 5.Nc3 {White’s best response is to keep developing with 5.Nc3, often followed by Na4 ideas. You’ll see this in about 38% of games. We continue developing normally with 5...Ne7.} 5...Ne7 6.Bd3 {Developing the light-squared bishop to d3 is best, and it’s played about 13% of the time. We castle our king, a natural move. Though in practice Black has scored well with 6...b5!?.} 6...O-O 7.Nge2 {Developing with 7.Nge2 has been the most common continuation. We continue developing while attacking e5. 7...Ng6 is next.} 7...Ng6 8.f4 {White defends e5 with their f-pawn. Our next move is to jump forward with our g-knight, 8...Nh4, a nice idea to attack White's king.} 8...Nh4 9.Kf1 {9.Kf1 is forced since 9. g3?? Nf3+ 10. Kf1 Bh3 is mate!} 9...f6 {Our final move in the variation is 9...f6, blowing up more files to attack White. I like Black's attack more than White's pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Fantasy Variation (3...e5) / Study 1: The Wild 3...e5 Countergambit / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/c326651a-49d6-427e-8d04-b4ec2840d52f/chapters/a78836b0-79c4-483a-9b2a-bac178d52a1d/studies/660a9eba-2cf9-4fbc-950c-74e0831d221f/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we deal with the Fantasy Variation, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro–Kann. I want to show you the 3...e5 weapon against it. White begins by occupying the center, and we respond in true Caro style.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White grabs space in the center with 2.d4. We counter with 2...d5, building our Caro pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White plays 3.f3, a funny-looking idea, but that’s the Fantasy! White refuses to give up the e4–d4 duo, even using the f-pawn to support it. We respond sharply with 3...e5!, aiming to eliminate the d4 pawn and exploit the weakened g1–a7 diagonal.} 3...e5 4.c3 {White's move really takes White’s strategy to the extreme, but the center becomes too fragile to hold. It appears in about 9% of games. We immediately strike back, breaking the center by capturing on d4.} 4...exd4 5.cxd4 {White recaptures, trying to keep the pawns connected. But we continue with yet another trade, 5...dxe4, further breaking down the center.} 5...dxe4 6.fxe4 {White has succeeded in keeping the two pawns together. But have they really?} 6...Qh4+ {After we check the king with the queen, White loses the e-pawn!} *